# Normalize line endings.
#
# Git stores text with LF and checks it out with whatever your OS prefers.
# Without this file, editing a shell script on Windows can commit CRLF line
# endings, and a Mac or Linux user who then clones the repo gets
# "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M" and cannot run the installer.
#
# These rules make that impossible regardless of who commits from where.

* text=auto

# Always LF — these are executed by a shell, and CRLF breaks them.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.command       text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf

# Always CRLF — Windows batch files need it.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf

# Never touch these — byte-for-byte, on every platform.
#
# The benchmark bundle is content-addressed: each file's name is the first 16
# hex characters of the sha256 of its own contents, and `scripts/replay_benchmark.py`
# re-derives that hash to prove the retained prompt is the one the pipeline
# generates. Line-ending normalization silently breaks exactly that: under
# `* text=auto` these files are stored with LF and checked out with CRLF on
# Windows, so a fresh Windows clone would hold prompts that no longer hash to
# their own names. The replay would fail there and pass on Linux, which is the
# worst version of the bug the bundle exists to prevent.
benchmarks/**  -text

*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.pptx          binary
*.docx          binary
*.xlsx          binary
*.zip           binary
# The S-1 corpus is byte-hashed evidence, exactly like benchmarks: CRLF
# conversion on a Windows checkout changed the raw bytes and made a fresh
# clone fail its own provenance check (external audit finding).
market-corpus/** -text
